.TH "CONDINAPT" "1" "October 2025" "CondinAPT Package Manager" ""
.SH NAME
condinapt \- conditional package installation tool for Debian\-like
systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]condinapt\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]CondinAPT\f[R] is a versatile tool for automating package
installation in any Debian\-like system (Debian, Ubuntu, and their
derivatives).
Its key feature is the ability to define complex conditions and rules
for installing each package based on arbitrary system configurations.
.SS Areas of Application
.IP \[bu] 2
Linux distribution build systems
.IP \[bu] 2
Automation of server and workstation setup
.IP \[bu] 2
Deployment of various system configurations
.IP \[bu] 2
Package management in Docker containers
.IP \[bu] 2
CI/CD pipelines for environment setup
.IP \[bu] 2
Creation of custom installation images
.SS Key Features
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Conditional Installation:\f[R] Install packages based on flexible
filters (+, \-)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]External Configuration:\f[R] Complete separation of logic (package
list) from data (system parameters)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Installation Queues:\f[R] Divide the process into sequential stages
to resolve dependencies
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Priority Queue:\f[R] Guaranteed installation of critical packages
first
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Complex Logic:\f[R] Support for \[lq]AND\[rq] (&&), \[lq]OR\[rq]
(||) operators, as well as group filters (+{a|b}, \-{a&b})
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Readability:\f[R] Support for comments and empty lines to structure
lists
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Backward Compatibility:\f[R] Supports simple package lists without
conditions
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R], \f[B]\-\-package\-list\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
(Required) Path to the package list file
.TP
\f[B]\-c\f[R], \f[B]\-\-config\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
(Required) Path to the main configuration file
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-\-filter\-mapping\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
(Optional) Path to the filter mapping file
.TP
\f[B]\-P\f[R], \f[B]\-\-priority\-list\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
(Optional) Path to a priority filter file.
File contains regex patterns to match packages.
Matched packages are moved to priority queue (preserving filters)
.TP
\f[B]\-s\f[R], \f[B]\-\-simulation\f[R]
Simulation mode.
Packages will not be installed
.TP
\f[B]\-C\f[R], \f[B]\-\-check\-only\f[R]
Only check if packages are already installed.
Returns exit code 1 if there are uninstalled packages.
At the end, outputs a command to install missing packages
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Verbose output
.TP
\f[B]\-vv\f[R], \f[B]\-\-very\-verbose\f[R]
Very verbose output
.TP
\f[B]\-x\f[R], \f[B]\-\-xtrace\f[R]
Enable \f[B]set \-x\f[R] command tracing
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\-\-force\f[R]
Force package lists update before installation.
By default, update is skipped if \f[B]/var/cache/apt/pkgcache.bin\f[R]
exists
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Show help
.SH CORE COMPONENTS
CondinAPT operates with four key files:
.SS condinapt script
The core, containing all processing logic
.SS Main configuration file (\-c)
A file with bash variables describing the current environment
.PP
Example (\f[B]system.conf\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
DISTRIBUTION=\[dq]bookworm\[dq]
SYSTEM_TYPE=\[dq]server\[dq]
ENVIRONMENT=\[dq]production\[dq]
LOCALE=\[dq]en_US\[dq]
FEATURES=\[dq]web,database\[dq]
.EE
.SS Filter mapping file (\-m)
Links short prefixes (used in the package list) to variable names from
the main configuration file.
This file is \f[B]optional\f[R].
If a filter is not present in the filter mapping file, it will be used
as a variable name from the main configuration file.
If the variable is not found, CondinAPT will declare it empty.
.PP
Example (\f[B]filters.map\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
d=DISTRIBUTION
st=SYSTEM_TYPE
env=ENVIRONMENT
arch=ARCHITECTURE
feat=FEATURES
.EE
.SS Package list file (\-l)
The main file describing what to install and under what conditions
.SH PACKAGE LIST SYNTAX
Each line in the package list file consists of two main parts:
.IP "1." 3
Package name with optional version and release specification
.IP "2." 3
Condition filters \- define the conditions under which the package will
be installed
.SS Package Name Structure
\f[B]Simple name:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
vim
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Package version:\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]package=version\f[R] \- loose version requirement.
If the required version is unavailable, an available version is
installed
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]package==version\f[R] \- strict requirement.
If the version is not found, installation aborts with an error
.PP
\f[B]Release specification:\f[R]
.PP
The release is specified using the **\[at]** symbol:
.IP
.EX
telegram\[at]bookworm\-backports
kernel\-image\-6.5.0\[at]trixie\-backports
.EE
.SS File Structure
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Package names:\f[R] Each package or condition is written on a new
line
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Comments:\f[R] Lines starting with \f[B]#\f[R], or text after
\f[B]#\f[R] on a line, are completely ignored
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Empty lines:\f[R] Ignored and serve for visual separation
.SS Filters and Conditions
.SS Single Filters
\f[B]+ (Positive):\f[R] The condition is true if the variable value
\f[B]matches\f[R]
.PP
Format: \f[B]+=\f[R]
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
nginx +st=server
.EE
.PP
\f[B]\- (Negative):\f[R] The condition is true if the variable value
\f[B]does not match\f[R]
.PP
Format: \f[B]\-=\f[R]
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
monitoring\-tools \-st=desktop
.EE
.SS Group Filters
\f[B]+{a|b} (OR for inclusion):\f[R] True if \f[B]at least one\f[R] of
the conditions in the group is true
.IP
.EX
web\-server +{st=server|st=web\-server}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]+{a&b} (AND for inclusion):\f[R] True only if \f[B]all\f[R]
conditions in the group are true
.IP
.EX
database\-tools +{d=bookworm&st=server}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]\-{a|b} (OR for exclusion):\f[R] The package is excluded if \f[B]at
least one\f[R] of the conditions is true
.IP
.EX
debug\-tools \-{env=production|st=minimal}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]\-{a&b} (AND for exclusion):\f[R] The package is excluded only if
\f[B]all\f[R] conditions are true
.IP
.EX
development\-tools \-{env=production&st=minimal}
.EE
.SS Logical Operators
\f[B]|| (OR / Fallback):\f[R] Try to install the left part.
If it fails, try the right part
.IP
.EX
exfatprogs \-d=bookworm || exfat\-utils
.EE
.PP
\f[B]&& (AND / Conjunction):\f[R] All parts must successfully pass
filter checks
.IP
.EX
nginx +st=web\-server && php\-fpm
.EE
.SS Special Modifiers
\f[B]!
(Mandatory Package):\f[R] If a package is marked with \f[B]!\f[R], but
cannot be found in repositories, CondinAPT will abort execution with an
error
.IP
.EX
!essential\-package
.EE
.PP
\f[B]\[at] (Release Specification):\f[R] Install a package from a
specific Debian/Ubuntu release
.IP
.EX
kernel\-image\-6.5.0 \[at]trixie\-backports
.EE
.SS Installation Queues
The \f[B]\[em]\f[R] separator on a separate line divides the list into
groups (queues).
Packages from one queue are installed together in a single apt call.
Queues are executed strictly sequentially.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
# Queue 1: Base system
systemd
network\-manager
\-\-\-
# Queue 2: Web server
nginx
php\-fpm
\-\-\-
# Queue 3: Monitoring
prometheus
.EE
.SS Priority Queue
The file specified by the \f[B]\-P\f[R] flag contains regex patterns
(one pattern per line, no filters).
CondinAPT scans all queues, finds packages matching these patterns, and
moves them (with all their filters and conditions) to a special
\[lq]Priority Queue\[rq], which is executed first.
.PP
\f[B]Pattern Matching:\f[R] Uses bash regex matching (=\[ti] operator).
Patterns can be simple package names or complex regex expressions.
.PP
\f[B]Preserving Context:\f[R] This mechanism preserves all package
conditions, filters, and release specifications from the original
package list.
.PP
\f[B]Override:\f[R] Matched packages are automatically removed from
their original queues (both regular and target queues with
\f[B]\[at]release\f[R]) and moved to priority queues.
Target releases are preserved in separate priority target queues.
.PP
Example \f[B]priority.list\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\[ha]linux\-.*
\[ha]firmware\-.*
\[ha]dkms$
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Quick Start
Create the configuration file \f[B]config.conf\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
DISTRIBUTION=\[dq]bookworm\[dq]
SYSTEM_TYPE=\[dq]server\[dq]
ENVIRONMENT=\[dq]production\[dq]
.EE
.PP
Create the package list \f[B]packages.list\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
# Base packages \- always installed
vim
curl

# Packages only for servers
nginx +SYSTEM_TYPE=server
mysql\-server +SYSTEM_TYPE=server

# Exclude packages for production environment
debug\-tools \-ENVIRONMENT=production
.EE
.PP
Run the installation:
.IP
.EX
condinapt \-l packages.list \-c config.conf
.EE
.PP
Or test in simulation mode:
.IP
.EX
condinapt \-l packages.list \-c config.conf \-s
.EE
.SS Multimedia Server
\f[B]packages.list:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
# Basic multimedia codecs \- always
gstreamer1.0\-plugins\-base
gstreamer1.0\-plugins\-good

# Additional codecs \- not for minimal installation
gstreamer1.0\-plugins\-bad \-st=minimal
gstreamer1.0\-plugins\-ugly \-st=minimal

# Professional tools \- only for full configuration
ffmpeg +st=media\-server
vlc +st=media\-server
\-\-\-
# Distribution\-specific packages from backports
ffmpeg \[at]bookworm\-backports +d=bookworm
.EE
.SS Web Server with Various Configurations
\f[B]packages.list:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
# Basic web server components
nginx
openssl

# Database \- only for full installations
mysql\-server +st=full\-server \-{env=minimal}
postgresql +st=database\-server

# PHP \- for web servers
php\-fpm +feat=php
php\-mysql +{feat=php&st=full\-server}

# Monitoring \- not for development
prometheus\-node\-exporter \-env=development
.EE
.SH OPERATING MODES
.SS Simulation Mode
Allows you to see which packages will be installed without actually
installing them:
.IP
.EX
condinapt \-l packages.list \-c system.conf \-s
.EE
.PP
In simulation mode, the script exits with exit code 1.
.SS Check Mode
Checks which packages from the list are already installed on the system:
.IP
.EX
condinapt \-l packages.list \-c system.conf \-C
.EE
.PP
Shows errors for uninstalled packages and returns exit code 1 if there
are uninstalled packages.
.SS Debugging Modes
\f[B]Verbose Output (\-v):\f[R]
.PP
Shows detailed information about filter checks and displays results for
each package.
.PP
\f[B]Very Verbose Output (\-vv):\f[R]
.PP
Maximum process detail, shows all intermediate steps.
.PP
\f[B]Command Tracing (\-x):\f[R]
.PP
Enables \f[B]set \-x\f[R] for script debugging, shows each command being
executed.
.SH ERROR HANDLING
.SS Mandatory Packages
If a package is marked as mandatory (\f[B]!\f[R]) but not found in
repositories, CondinAPT:
.IP "1." 3
Outputs an error message
.IP "2." 3
Aborts execution (unless in simulation mode)
.IP "3." 3
Returns exit code 1
.SS Handling Unavailable Versions
\f[B]Loose Versions (=):\f[R]
.PP
If the exact version is unavailable, any available version is installed
with a warning.
.PP
\f[B]Strict Versions (==):\f[R]
.PP
If the exact version is unavailable, the package is skipped.
If the package is mandatory (\f[B]!\f[R]), execution aborts.
.SS Version Holding
CondinAPT automatically applies \f[B]apt\-mark hold\f[R] when the
exactly requested version was installed, preventing automatic updates.
